        <section class="guide-card" aria-labelledby="plan-install">
          <span class="eyebrow">04 · Plan beyond the blocks</span>
          <h2 id="plan-install">The base and joints need their own specification</h2>
          <p>Piece count does not determine how much crushed stone, bedding, mortar, or joint fill to buy. Those materials depend on the selected assembly and on traffic, soil strength, drainage, climate, and block thickness. Natural-stone manufacturer details show different base and setting systems; copying one bedding depth into every cobble driveway can misstate the job. Have your installer specify the section and joint product before estimating those materials.</p>
          <ol class="check-list">
            <li><strong>Confirm the stone and orientation.</strong> Ask which face lies flat for paving or points along the curb, and whether the quoted size is nominal or actual.</li>
            <li><strong>Set the joint and pattern.</strong> Joint width changes the count. Rough split and reclaimed faces may need trial layout and sorting. Match the filler to its approved joint-width range.</li>
            <li><strong>Specify the structure.</strong> Confirm the subgrade, drainage, compacted base, bedding, edge restraint, and vehicle loads. A raised curb and a flat apron can need different details.</li>
            <li><strong>Order and stage delivery.</strong> Check full or partial pallet rules, total shipping weight, unloading space, and whether extra pieces should be kept for repairs.</li>
          </ol>
          <div class="related-callout">
            <strong>Estimating the supporting layer?</strong>
